{{also|sekt}}
==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|de|Sect||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed in the 17th century, and provided with a [[paragogic]] ''-t'', from {{bor|de|fr|sec|(vin) sec|lit=dry wine}}, which is also the original sense in German. The sense “sparkling wine” is believed to originate from an anecdote in 19th-century Berlin. The actor {{w|Ludwig Devrient}} supposedly ordered a bottle of wine using the phrase “{{lang|de|Bring [er] mir Sekt, Schurke!}}”, based on the German translation of the line “Give me a cup of {{l|en|sack|id=wine}}, rogue!” from Shakespeare’s ''{{w|Henry IV, Part 1|Henry IV}}''. He was served sparkling wine, his usual order, and this sense was given to the word {{m|de||Sekt}} when the phrase and anecdote caught on.

